{"points": [{"heading": "What is a fraction?", "text": "A fraction shows equal parts of one whole. The bottom number tells how many parts. The top tells how many parts we take."}, {"heading": "Numerator and denominator", "text": "Numerator is the top number. Denominator is the bottom number. They work together to name one amount."}, {"heading": "Equivalent fractions look the same", "text": "Different fractions can show the same amount. We call these equivalent fractions. Use bar models of the same size to compare them."}, {"heading": "Make equivalent fractions", "text": "Multiply both the numerator and denominator by the same number. The fraction looks different but shows the same amount."}, {"heading": "Compare with bar models", "text": "Draw bar models the same length. Shade the parts each fraction shows. The longer shaded part is the bigger fraction."}, {"heading": "Order fractions from smallest to largest", "text": "Compare two fractions at a time using bar models. Write them in a line from smallest to largest or largest to smallest."}, {"heading": "Watch the denominator carefully", "text": "When denominators are different, do not compare only the bottom number. Draw bar models to see which fraction is truly bigger."}], "formulas": [{"label": "Make an equivalent fraction", "rule": "Multiply the numerator by a number, and multiply the denominator by the same number.", "example": "1/2 = (1×2)/(2×2) = 2/4. Both numerator and denominator must change."}, {"label": "Check if fractions are equivalent", "rule": "Draw two bar models the same size. If the shaded parts cover the same area, the fractions are equivalent.", "example": "1/2 and 2/4 shade the same area when bars are the same length."}, {"label": "Compare fractions with unlike denominators", "rule": "Draw bar models of equal length for each fraction. Shade the parts. Compare the shaded areas, not just the numbers.", "example": "3/4 is bigger than 1/3 because the shaded part of 3/4 is larger when both bars are the same size."}, {"label": "Order fractions", "rule": "Use bar models to compare all fractions two at a time. Arrange them from smallest to largest or largest to smallest.", "example": "Order 1/2, 1/4, and 3/4: smallest to largest is 1/4, 1/2, 3/4."}]}
